Z||000004||StuGood||11-29-2001||10:19 AM||||Hmmm...I forget some of the details of the relay circuitry right now.  However, I'm thinking you might want to try swapping out the isolation relay w/ a known good one - assuming you have another one around somewhere...?||||reg||5
Z||000005||Rodrv6||11-29-2001||11:15 AM||||Maybe the magnet in the motor got turned around?  I did that once and the motor operated up only.  Turns out the magnet had rotated 180 degrees around the armature when I reassembled it.<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Turbo88||11-29-2001||12:16 PM||||I just did the Rodney Dickman rebuild on mine and found the same problem. I'll bet ya you hooked up the linkage wrong. I did the same thing on my and it didn't want to go up or down either. Try changing your headlight to motor linkage around (it will only hook up 2 ways I belive). Let us know if that fixes the problem.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Turbo88||11-29-2001||12:17 PM||||Two Posts!<p>[This message has been edited by Turbo88 (edited 11-29-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||11-29-2001||05:47 PM||||I just messed with it for the past couple of hours.  I took the motor out of the assy and tried it.  It works fine in both directions but as soon as I put it back in the assy and install the assy back in the car, it only goes up.  All I can think of is that the linkage (from the S shaped connecting rod to the motor itself) is wrong.  I've hand cranked it (with the assy together) and it will go up and down easily.  I don't know what else to do.  I'm ready to sell the car (way too frustrated to keep it at this point) but I'd like to get this figured out before I do.||||reg||1
Z||000009||conductor||11-29-2001||06:40 PM||||Your ECM has a virus  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ,get the latest Norton.<BR>Just kidding, hope you find out your problem soon.<BR>There must be some kind of a relay in the drivers wheelwell, isnt that one giving you a hard time? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Grtx Richard.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Monkeyman||11-29-2001||07:27 PM||||Rodrv6--I think I love you.  Apparently you hit the nail on the head.  I disassembled the motor (for the millionth time), turned the magnet around (I assume it's the oval/round metal piece that surrounds the coiled wire?) and reassembled everything.  Reinstalled it in my car and it works.  (Do you know how difficult it is to work on a car in the dark?  Good thing I've done this once or twice (or a million times).)  Thank you so much for your suggestion.  I've rebuilt headlight motors before and never had this problem.   Guess I was just lucky before, eh?<P>Thanx to all who replied.  Even if some of the suggestions didn't work, it's nice to know that you were willing to help.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Rodrv6||11-29-2001||08:50 PM||||Awwww, geeeeee  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Just glad to help.  When it happened to me I about went nuts trying to figure out what had happened.  Unfortunatly, this forum didn't exist then!!!!<P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  <BR>White 88 GT :)||||reg||1
